DHCP is to think of it as the service that automatically gives IP addresses to computers, while AD handles users, computers, authentication, and policies.

1. First understand: AD and DHCP are different

In a typical Windows environment, you may have:

Windows Server
│
├── Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS)
│      └── Manages users and computers
│
├── DNS
│      └── Resolves names to IP addresses
│
└── DHCP
       └── Automatically gives IP addresses

They are different services, but they commonly work together.

For example:

Domain: corp.example

Server:
IP: 192.168.1.10

Client PC:
DHCP → gets 192.168.1.20

