3. Example of a real company

Suppose you create this domain:

corp.example

Your Windows Server has:

Server Name: DC01
IP Address: 192.168.1.10

You install:

AD DS
DNS
DHCP

Your DHCP configuration might be:

Scope: 192.168.1.100 - 192.168.1.200

Subnet Mask:
255.255.255.0

Gateway:
192.168.1.1

DNS Server:
192.168.1.10

Domain:
corp.example

Now a client computer joins the network.

4. Client PC starts

The PC doesn't know its IP.

It sends:

DHCP Discover

DHCP responds:

IP Address:
192.168.1.100

Subnet Mask:
255.255.255.0

Gateway:
192.168.1.1

DNS:
192.168.1.10

Domain:
corp.example

Now the PC has network connectivity.

7. Then the PC joins Active Directory

For eg

Client PC
    │
    │ Join Domain
    ↓
corp.example
    │
    ↓
Domain Controller
    │
    ↓
Computer account created

The computer gets an account inside Active Directory.

For example:

Active Directory
│
└── Computers
     │
     ├── PC01
     ├── PC02
     └── PC03

8. Very important: DHCP doesn't join the PC to AD

This is a common misunderstanding.

DHCP:

Gives IP configuration

AD:

Manages users/computers

DNS:

Helps computers find each other and AD services

So:

DHCP
 ↓
Gives PC an IP
 ↓
DNS
 ↓
Finds Domain Controller
 ↓
AD
 ↓
Authenticates/manages the computer and user

9. What happens when a user logs in?

Suppose your user is:

bilal

They enter:

Username: bilal
Password: ********
Domain: corp.example

The computer communicates with the Domain Controller.

PC01
  │
  │ "I need to authenticate bilal"
  ↓
DNS
  │
  ↓
Domain Controller
  │
  ↓
Active Directory
  │
  ↓
User authentication
  │
  ↓
Access granted

DHCP is not involved in checking the password.

10. One important Windows Server point

When you install the DHCP Server role on Windows Server, in an AD domain environment you normally need to authorize the DHCP server in Active Directory.

Why?

To prevent an unauthorized DHCP server from distributing incorrect IP configuration on the domain network.

Think:

DHCP Server
     ↓
"Can I provide DHCP services?"
     ↓
Active Directory
     ↓
Authorized ✓
     ↓
DHCP can operate

An unauthorized DHCP server can cause serious network problems because clients might receive the wrong:

IP
Gateway
DNS
